          "graphs": {
            "anyOf": [
              {
                "items": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/GraphItem"
                },
                "type": "array"
              },
              {
                "type": "null"
              }
            ],
            "title": "Graphs"
          },
          "output_encoding": {
            "anyOf": [
              {
                "$ref": "#/components/schemas/RequestOutputEncoding"
              },
              {
                "type": "null"
              }
            ],
            "description": "How response tensors are encoded: 'json' for nested lists, 'base64' for {\"data\": <base64 little-endian bytes>, \"dtype\": ..., \"shape\": [...]} per output, 'safetensors' for an application/vnd.safetensors response body (the same as sending Accept: application/vnd.safetensors). Omit to use the server default (--output-encoding / DOWNSHIFT_OUTPUT_ENCODING)."
          }
        },
        "type": "object",
        "title": "GraphPredictRequest",
        "description": "One graph (`x`, `edge_index` and optional `edge_attr` at the top level) or a batch\n(`graphs`, a list of the same three fields). Never both.\n\nA batch runs as one inference over the graphs, which downshift joins into one disjoint\ngraph. Each `edge_index` uses the local node IDs of its own graph, and the server adds the\noffsets. The response is {\"graphs\": [{outputs, shapes, dtypes}, ...]} in the order of the\nrequest. With `Accept: application/vnd.safetensors`, each output is a tensor with the name\n`graphs.<i>.<output name>`, and `__metadata__` has the number of graphs as\n`downshift.graphs`. Outputs at node level and edge level are split for each graph. A model\nwhose output has a fixed size (a pooled readout) takes one graph for each request.\nA safetensors request body can carry the batch as joined `x`, `edge_index` and `edge_attr`,\nplus the int64 vectors `num_nodes` and `num_edges` of length G.\nEach tensor is a nested list or a TypedArray dict (this is how base64 arrives)."
      },
